  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & ={e}^{2(1)}+C \\\\ {e}^{2} & ={e}^{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)={e}^{2t}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+K \\\\ {e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+K \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\n$$",
